{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet you only get at 6:12 a.m. in a city that hasn't decided to wake up yet.\n\nThe streetlights are still on but they're embarrassed about it. The coffee shop on the corner is open, not because it expects customers, but because Luis always opens at six. He's wiping the same counter he wiped last night and will wipe again tomorrow. The light inside makes the sidewalk look warm.\n\nI like being early to the world. Not early to appointments \u2014 I'm chronically five minutes late to those \u2014 but early to the day itself. Before the emails and the headlines and the group chats start buzzing. When your brain is still slightly soft around the edges, not yet hardened into all its opinions.\n\nIn that window, everything feels reversible. You could become a person who runs in the morning. You could learn to bake sourdough or finally read that book on naval history you bought to impress someone three years ago. You could move to Lisbon. The possibilities haven't been narrowed down by the actual choices of the day yet.\n\nOf course, by 9 a.m. most of that evaporates. You become, again, the person who drinks too much coffee and forgets to water the plants.\n\nBut I think it's important to visit that early, possibility-rich version of the morning. Even if you don't do anything with it. Just to remember that your life isn't as fixed as it feels at 2 p.m. on a Tuesday.",
